 "key_big": "√ab = √a × √b",
 "key_sub": "Rationalise: multiply top and bottom by the surd.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Find the biggest square factor: √72 = √36 × √2 = 6√2.</li><li>Only like surds can be added: 3√2 + 5√2 = 8√2.</li><li>To rationalise 1/(a + √b), multiply top and bottom by a − √b.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>√50 + √18</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>5√2 + 3√2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 8√2</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>6/√3</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>× √3/√3</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 2√3</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>√(a + b) = √a + √b</td><td>That is false: √(9 + 16) = 5, not 7.</td></tr><tr><td>Adding unlike surds</td><td>√2 + √3 cannot be simplified.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting to multiply the top when rationalising</td><td>Multiply top and bottom.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is a surd?",
   "a": "A root that is not a whole number, like √2."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is √3 × √3?",
   "a": "3."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the conjugate of 2 + √5?",
   "a": "2 − √5."
  }
